# edocore architecture

`Assets/edocore` is the root of the framework. Each layer has `Scripts/` and `Datas/` subfolders.

| Folder | Assembly | Purpose |
|--------|----------|---------|
| `Assets/edocore/Core` | `edocore.Core` | Internal implementation. Game makers must never touch it. |
| `Assets/edocore/External` | `edocore.External` | Public API that game makers call directly. |
| `Assets/edocore/Tools` | `edocore.Tools` | Generic tools that work immediately (AsynchronousTask, logs, maths...). |
| `Assets/edocore/Sample` | `edocore.Sample` | Files used to try the framework. Behaves like a game maker. |

## Allowed dependencies

- Scripts outside `Assets/edocore` (Assembly-CSharp) -> External and Tools only.
- Sample -> External and Tools only.
- External -> Core, Tools.
- Core -> Tools only (never External, never Sample).
- Tools -> nothing in edocore.
- Nothing may depend on Sample.

## Assembly definitions

- Never add `edocore.Core` to the references of any asmdef other than `edocore.External`.
- `autoReferenced`: false on `edocore.Core` and `edocore.Sample`; true on `edocore.External` and `edocore.Tools`.
- External never stores a Core type in a field or exposes one in a public/protected signature; Core types are only used inside method bodies or `internal` members. Core receives settings as plain data, never as External types.

## Entry points

- External: `Starter`, `Parameters`, `ServiceParameter`, `Service`, `Service_Actor`, `Framework`.
- Core: `Router`, `ServicesHandler` (reached through `Router.Current.Services`).
- Tools: `AsynchronousTask` and future generic tools.
- Game makers extend the framework by subclassing `Starter`, `Service<...>`, `Service_Actor` and `ServiceParameter`.

## Services (Strategy, terminate before switching)

- `Service_X : Service<Service_X_Actor>` or `Service<Service_X_Actor, Service_X_Parameter>`: commands and feedbacks only.
- `Service_X_Actor : Service_Actor` (or `Service_Actor<Service_X_Parameter>`): abstract operations every implementation provides.
- `Service_X_Actor_Y`: the only place third-party SDK code goes.
- `Service_X_Parameter : ServiceParameter`: settings asset, listed in `Parameters`.
- Core never references concrete service types; it talks to services through callbacks and listeners.
